# SIGNAL(2)

## NAME

signal - catch or ignore signals

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <signal.h>

void (*signal(int sig, void (*func)(int)))(int);
```

## DESCRIPTION

A signal is generated by some abnormal event, initiated either by user at a typewriter (quit,
interrupt), by a program error (bus error, etc.), or by request of another program (kill).
Normally all signals cause termination of the receiving process, but a *signal* call allows
them either to be ignored or to cause an interrupt to a specified location. Here is the list of
signals with names as in the include file.

```
SIGHUP  1       hangup
SIGINT  2       interrupt
SIGQUIT 3*      quit
SIGILL  4*      illegal instruction (not reset when caught)
SIGTRAP 5*      trace trap (not reset when caught)
SIGABRT 6*      abort(3); v7 called it SIGIOT
SIGEMT  7*      EMT instruction
SIGFPE  8*      floating point exception
SIGKILL 9       kill (cannot be caught or ignored)
SIGBUS  10*     bus error
SIGSEGV 11*     segmentation violation
SIGSYS  12*     bad argument to system call
SIGPIPE 13      write on a pipe or link with no one to read it
SIGALRM 14      alarm clock
SIGTERM 15      software termination signal
        16      unassigned
```

The starred signals in the list above cause a core image if not caught or ignored.

**Note:** the numbers are v7's, all fifteen, and NSIG stays 17. C11 sec 7.14 requires only six
of them and permits the rest. **Signal 6 is SIGABRT and nothing else:** v7 called it SIGIOT,
after a PDP-11 instruction this machine has not got, and that name is not defined here - the
number is still v7's, and abort(3) raises it. Every signal above has exactly one name; the
kernel spells them the way this page does.

**There is no job control here.** No SIGTSTP, no SIGWINCH, no signal set or mask; the list
above is the whole of it.

If *func* is SIG_DFL, the default action for signal *sig* is reinstated; this default is
termination, sometimes with a core image. If *func* is SIG_IGN the signal is ignored. Otherwise
when the signal occurs *func* will be called with the signal number as argument. **Note:** v7
declared **int (\*signal())();** - no prototype at all, a handler returning **int**, and a
SIG_DFL cast to that shape. **A handler takes the signal number and returns void here,** per
C11 sec 7.14.1.1, and that is the shape the kernel's frame actually delivers. A return from the
function will continue the process at the point it was interrupted. Except as indicated, a
signal is reset to SIG_DFL after being caught. Thus if it is desired to catch every such
signal, the catching routine must issue another *signal* call.

When a caught signal occurs during certain system calls, the call terminates prematurely. In
particular this can occur during a *read* or write(2) on a slow device (like a typewriter; but
not a file); and during *pause* or wait(2). When such a signal occurs, the saved user status is
arranged in such a way that when return from the signal-catching takes place, it will appear
that the system call returned an error status. The user's program may then, if it wishes,
re-execute the call.

The value of *signal* is the previous (or initial) value of *func* for the particular signal.

After a fork(2) the child inherits all signals. Exec(2) resets all caught signals to default
action.

## SEE ALSO

kill(1), kill(2), ptrace(2), setjmp(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

The value (int)-1 is returned if the given signal is out of range.

## BUGS

If a repeated signal arrives before the last one can be reset, there is no chance to catch it.

**Note:** v7's second bug - "the type specification of the routine and its *func* argument are
problematical" - is fixed, not carried. See above.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The return path is the kernel's, so libc has none of the usual machinery

The kernel copies the whole trap frame onto the user stack and **plants the return path in
it:** a **$77 SYS_sigret** word, copied out above the frame and named as the handler's return
address, so a handler's ordinary return trips an extracode and the kernel reloads the frame.

Everything that follows is an absence. **The signal stub stays the plain generated leaf.**
There is no trampoline of the kind other ports carry, no libc table shadowing the kernel's
dispositions, and **signal() therefore still answers with the true previous disposition** -
including the SIG_DFL that a caught signal is reset to on delivery. Libc gained exactly one
routine when delivery landed: raise(3).

**It could not have been libc's work in any case.** Which *half* of a word to resume at lives
in the saved program status word, and only the privileged return instruction restores it, so a
handler interrupted from a right-half instruction can be resumed only by the kernel.

### A handler is entered by the ordinary one-argument convention

The number in the accumulator, r14 at -1, and the mode register at 7 - the value *crt0* would
otherwise have established, which a handler has no opportunity to.

A handler may leave by longjmp(3) instead of returning; sleep(3) does exactly that, and it is
why setjmp(3) saves the mode register at all.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_signal** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
